    "narrative": "LawDog\u2019s lineup for June 15 is anchored by **Geraldo Perdomo** (66.66) and **Lawrence Butler** (64.73), our two strongest plays of the day. **Geraldo Perdomo** benefits from a high-efficiency matchup against Ure\u00f1a, where a +16% boost for the pitcher's skill profile and a favorable park factor create an ideal environment for his offensive floor. Meanwhile, **Lawrence Butler** is our primary source of upside; despite a suboptimal #7 spot in the order, his +10% park factor and +13% platoon edge against Jones make him a mandatory start to chase those scarce stolen bases and power stats. **Bryson Stott** (62.55) remains a pillar of the middle infield, as his ability to exploit Gusto\u2019s slow delivery significantly enhances his stolen base potential, justifying his position as a high-tier start despite a moderate skill assessment of the opposing arm.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved balancing the sub-60 scores of **Jonathan Aranda** (57.13), **Tyler Freeman** (57.58), **Gavin Sheets** (51.77), and **Masyn Winn** (51.87) against our bench depth. While **Jonathan Aranda** and **Tyler Freeman** posted the highest scores among the \"marginal\" cohort, their platoon risks against Lauer and Imanaga, respectively, capped their ceiling. Ultimately, we are leaning on **Masyn Winn** and **Gavin Sheets** to round out the back end of the roster, as the former benefits from a slow-delivery pitcher that creates a high-probability environment for a stolen base, which remains our primary KPI for roster construction. The active roster remains thin in elite talent due to the ongoing absences of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, **Cal Raleigh**, **Will Smith**, and **Daulton Varsho**, forcing us to rely on high-volume, efficiency-focused contributors like **Matt Chapman**, **Luis Arraez**, and **Brendan Donovan** to maintain our position in the 5x5 standings. We continue to monitor the status of **Taylor Ward**, **Ryan Waldschmidt**, **Dylan Beavers**, 